**xsc4** — identical to xs on C0–C4 fuels (within ±0.1 cm/s); no aromatic
coverage at all.

Reading of the curve: 111→66 species is nearly free on the primary matrix
(worst 14 %); 66→58 starts costing alkane lean/high-T accuracy (~24 %); below
~58 the greedy found nothing more to remove without breaking primary-fuel
screens (a 0.35-tolerance run converged to 57 with the same composition class),
i.e. the practical floor of this approach on the full USC-II target set is
≈57–58 species. Dropping aromatics entirely (xsc4) buys 10 more species with no
C0–C4 accuracy loss — but no benzene/toluene capability.

## 5. Consistency checks

* All three mechanisms load in Cantera 3.2; every reaction element-balanced
  (verified programmatically, 0 violations).
* Duplicate-reaction flags recomputed after filtering (a lone reaction may not
  keep the flag); third-body efficiency lists pruned of removed species only.
* Thermo/transport data untouched; the parent's NASA-polynomial mid-T
  discontinuity warnings (CH2OCH, C4H5-2, C4H6-2, C2H3CHOCH2, CH3CHCHCHO) are
  inherited verbatim where those species survive.
* No ignition-failure introduced: 0 no-ignition cases on the primary matrix for
  all three mechanisms (the 3 dry-CO no-ignition cases are inherited from the
  parent's own behavior).

## 6. Known limitations (honest list)

1. **Benzene (C6H6):** lite −49…+41 % IDT; xs up to +151 %. The greedy screen
   (design oversight) covered toluene but not benzene; post-hoc tests show the
   C5H6/lC5H7 subnetwork is so finely balanced that only a near-complete
   aromatic block restores it (~10 species, conflicting with the size target).
   Toluene — the more practically relevant aromatic — is retained at ≤20 % (lite)
   / ≤46 % (xs). Treat benzene IDT as outside the claimed accuracy.
2. **C3H6 (propene), xs/xsc4 only:** −25…−41 % SL, up to +57 % lean IDT; the
   C3H4/C3H5-isomer block was eliminated; reinstating 4 species only halves the
   error, so it was left out and is documented instead. lite keeps C3H6 at
   ≤21 % IDT / ≤3.6 cm/s SL.
3. **C2H2 rich flames:** −11.5 % SL worst-case for lite (C4 chemistry reduced);
   xs is coincidentally better here (2.9 %) through error cancellation.
4. **Lean, low-T corners** carry the largest IDT errors everywhere (by far the
   stiffest chemistry); errors are systematic in sign per fuel, not random.
5. Validation is against the **parent**, not against raw experiments (parent
   fidelity to experiment is a separate question — see §7 for a spot check).
   Dry-CO ignition is outside the validated domain (no-ignition in 2 s already
   in the parent).

## 7. Comparison with experimental data (external)

Independent literature check, compiled by a delegated researcher (lit-scout;
full citations, source quotes and URLs in `agents/lit-scout/literature_refs.json`
and `literature_refs.md`). These are **experimental measurements**, distinct
from the parent-model references used elsewhere in this report; they were not
used at any reduction or fitting step.

**Laminar burning velocity, fuel/air at 1 atm, 298 K (experimental values vs
parent prediction).** Experimental consensus from multiple independent
laboratories (Park/Egolfopoulos, Bosschaart/de Goey, Vagelopoulos, Jomaas/Law,
Hassan/Faeth, Taylor 1991; see citations):

| fuel, phi | experiment (cm/s) | parent (cm/s) | parent−exp |
|---|---|---|---|
| CH4, 1.0 | 35.9±1 / 36.5±2 / 34.7±2 (≈36) | 36.5 | within ±2 % |
| C2H4, 1.0 | 62.3–68.5 (≈65) | 63.8 | within spread |
| C3H8, 1.0 | 38.3–41.4 (≈39–40) | 39.6 | within spread |
| C2H6, 1.0 | 38.1–40.8 (≈40) | 44.1 | ≈ +9 % |
| CH3OH, 1.0 / 1.1 | 43.2±1 / 46.3±1 | 46.1 / 50.4 | ≈ +7…+9 % |
| H2, peak ≈1.7–1.8 | 280–285 | 296 | ≈ +4 % |
| C2H2/air, ≈1.1 | ~130–145 (**unsourced** here — canonical refs paywalled) | 133 | n/a |

**Ignition delay, shock tube.** Brabbs & Robertson, NASA TM-87268 (1986),
2 % CH4/4 % O2/94 % Ar, phi = 1.0 (experimental, pressure-trace): 1.448 ms at
1593 K / 2.76 atm and 0.794 ms at 1655 K / 2.93 atm. Computed at the same
conditions (constant-volume):

| model | 1593 K | 1655 K |
|---|---|---|
| experiment | 1.448 ms | 0.794 ms |
| parent | 0.920 ms (0.64×) | 0.489 ms (0.62×) |
| usc2_lite | 0.918 ms | 0.488 ms |
| usc2_xs | 0.986 ms (1.07× parent) | 0.527 ms (1.08× parent) |

Two conclusions: (i) the *parent itself* predicts ignition ≈35–40 % faster than
these shock-tube points (a property of USC-II and of the idealized reactor, not
of the reduction); (ii) `usc2_lite` reproduces the parent at these *out-of-
matrix* conditions (Ar diluent, ~3 atm) to <0.2 %, i.e. the reduced mechanism's
fidelity to the parent is not limited to the validation grid.

**Net:** the parent agrees with flame-speed experiments to within experimental
spread for CH4/C2H4/C3H8 and is ~4–9 % fast for H2/CH3OH/C2H6; our tolerance
frame (IDT ~25 %, SL ~5 %/2 cm/s reduced-vs-parent) is of the same order as
both experimental scatter and the parent's own deviation from experiment.

